{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, was born around 1135 and died on March 25, 1187. He was the second son of Melisende of Jerusalem and Fulk V, King of Jerusalem, and elder brother of Baldwin IV, King of Jerusalem. His father Fulk V died when he was young, and Raymond III succeeded his uncle, Baldwin of Ibelin, as Count of Tripoli in 1152.\n\n*highlighted section part 1* \n\nRaymond III's reign as Count of Tripoli was marked by his efforts to strengthen the Crusader states and to maintain their independence from the Byzantine Empire. He was a key figure in the defense of the Crusader states against various threats, including the rise of the Ayyubids under Saladin. Raymond III played a significant role in the political and military landscape of the Levant during the later half of the 12th century.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nIn the early 1170s, Raymond III led a campaign against the Assassins, a powerful Shia Islamic sect. The campaign was not successful, and he was captured by the Assassins and later released. This incident highlighted the complex and often volatile political environment in which Raymond III operated. The Assassins were known for their targeted killings of key figures, making their activities a significant concern for the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III died on March 25, 1187, during the Siege of Jerusalem by Saladin's forces. His death marked a significant loss for the Crusader states, as he was a prominent and influential leader. His legacy includes his efforts to strengthen the Crusader states and his role in the broader context of the Crusades. The loss of Raymond III was a blow to the Crusader efforts, especially as Saladin's forces were gaining momentum in their campaign to reclaim the Levant.\n\nRaymond III's life and reign were characterized by his attempts to navigate the complex political and military challenges of the time. Despite the setbacks he faced, including the unsuccessful campaign against the Assassins and the ultimate fall of Jerusalem, he is remembered as a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states. His leadership and strategic decisions were instrumental in shaping the destiny of the Crusader states during a critical period in their history.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.673804759979248}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00022063204960431904}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00040749352774582803}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.11399869620800018}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.013085562735795975}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.361645576660521e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.321732068201527e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.218599158572033e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.3725743889808655}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.5496751666069031}, {"token": " born", "logprob": -2.977053165435791}, {"token": " around", "logprob": -1.256922721862793}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.012936124578118324}, {"token": "113", "logprob": -0.9638474583625793}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.6611848473548889}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.507529616355896}, {"token": " died", "logprob": -0.8933879137039185}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -0.985293984413147}, {"token": " March", "logprob": -2.86037015914917}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -6.663577369181439e-05}, {"token": "25", "logprob": -0.7650099992752075}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0022082962095737457}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.075552376685664e-05}, {"token": "118", "logprob": -0.0013608013978227973}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -0.0009235645993612707}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.09586935490369797}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.03857937827706337}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.16155579686164856}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.9146300554275513}, {"token": " second", "logprob": -4.324182987213135}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.025720518082380295